.TH SM-MEMORY-PRUNE 1 2026-11-16 "sm-memory-prune 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sm-memory-prune \- find and forget near-duplicate supermemory memories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sm-memory-prune
.RB [ \-\-base
.IR url ]
.RB [ \-\-namespace
.IR tag ]
.RB [ \-\-threshold
.IR sim ]
.RB [ \-\-mode
.RI { lexical | semantic }]
.RB [ \-\-workers
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-apply ]
.RB [ \-\-apply-min-lexical
.IR sim ]
.RB [ \-\-max-clusters
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-show
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-cache
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-no-cache ]
.RB [ \-\-api-key
.IR key ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sm-memory-prune
reads every active memory in one supermemory namespace, groups near-duplicates,
and can forget the redundant copies.  Detection never calls an LLM, and
deletion uses the exact-ID forget endpoint, so a full prune costs zero model
tokens.
.P
Two detection modes exist.
.B lexical
(the default) hashes token sets with MinHash and verifies Jaccard similarity
locally; it only catches near-verbatim repeats but needs no server calls.
.B semantic
asks the running server for each memory's nearest neighbours through
.BR /v4/search ,
which uses the local embedding model (no LLM).  It is slower (roughly a
minute per thousand memories) but finds paraphrases.  Results are cached.
.P
Groups whose members differ in identifiers (numbers, dates, paths, backticked
code) are never merged, in either mode.  For
.BR \-\-apply ,
a group must additionally pass
.B \-\-apply-min-lexical
(its members must share most of their wording).  Groups that only match
semantically are reported under
.B review_only
and left alone, because near-identical phrasing can describe different facts
(for example the same sentence about an MCP server for RAG, email, image
generation and memory).
.P
Inside a mergable group the newest memory wins; a longer text breaks ties.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-base " url"
Server base URL (default
.BR http://localhost:6767 ,
or
.BR $SUPERMEMORY_BASE_URL ).
.TP
.BI \-\-namespace " tag"
Container tag to inspect (default
.BR sm_project_default ).
.TP
.BI \-\-threshold " sim"
Minimum similarity, 0\-1 (default
.BR 0.92 ).
Raise it to be stricter.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " lexical|semantic"
Detection strategy (default
.BR lexical ).
.TP
.BI \-\-workers " n"
Parallel requests in semantic mode (default
.BR 6 ).
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Forget the redundant memories in every
.B safe
group.  Without this flag nothing is written.
.TP
.BI \-\-apply-min-lexical " sim"
Minimum token-set overlap for
.BR \-\-apply
(default
.BR 0.85 ).
Groups below it are review-only.
.TP
.BI \-\-max-clusters " n"
Stop after
.I n
clusters (0, the default, means all).
.TP
.BI \-\-show " n"
Print this many clusters (default
.BR 10 ).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print clusters as JSON (winner id, forget ids, texts).
.TP
.BI \-\-cache " file"
Where semantic pairs are cached (default
.BR ~/.cache/sm-memory-prune/pairs.json ).
The cache is keyed by the namespace's memory ids and the threshold.
.TP
.B \-\-no-cache
Ignore any cached pairs and re-query the server.
.TP
.BI \-\-api-key " key"
API key; otherwise
.BR $SUPERMEMORY_API_KEY
or
.BR ~/.supermemory/api-key .
.SH EXAMPLES
Dry run over the live namespace with the fast local detector:
.EX
sm-memory-prune --threshold 0.92 --show 20
.EE
.P
Find paraphrases using the server's embeddings, then review:
.EX
sm-memory-prune --mode semantic --threshold 0.92 --show 20
.EE
.P
Apply only the safe, near-verbatim merges:
.EX
sm-memory-prune --mode semantic --threshold 0.92 --apply
.EE
.P
Export the shortlist for manual review:
.EX
sm-memory-prune --mode semantic --json > /tmp/prune.json
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.cache/sm-memory-prune/pairs.json
Cached semantic neighbour pairs.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
Forgetting is a soft delete: the memory is marked forgotten, removed from
recall and dropped from the vector index, but its audit row remains.  The
category is therefore recoverable from the local store, but not through the
API.
.SH NOTES
Deleting a document
.RB ( DELETE " /v3/documents/{id})"
cascades to the memories it sourced and is also free; it is the cheaper way to
remove an entire redundant capture, but it drops any facts only that capture
produced.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sm-llm-proxy (1),
.BR curl (1),
.BR jq (1)
